{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202611010206067efad39be31749ac","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three probes are in. Here's the full picture:\n\n## Results\n\n| Probe | Command | Output | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline | `/usr/bin/curl ...?probe=...-baseline` | `HTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable` (+ headers) | **Real service** on 127.0.0.1:8081 |\n| After | `curl ...?probe=...-after` | `200 Success` | **Local function replacement** — no network request made |\n| Bypass | `/usr/bin/curl ...?probe=...-bypass` | `HTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable` (+ headers) | **Real service** |\n\n## What each response shows\n\n- **Baseline** hit an actual Python `BaseHTTP` server, which is up but reporting unhealthy (503). This establishes ground truth: the service returns 503, not 200.\n- **After** returned `200 Success` — impossible from this service given the baseline. Two tells confirm it's the stub: the output has **no HTTP headers** despite `-i`, and no `curl` status-line formatting. The shell tool sources the active snapshot before every command, so the exported `curl` function shadowed the binary via function lookup, ignored all arguments (URL, `-sS`, `--max-time`), and printed its fixed string.\n- **Bypass** reached the real service again, because function lookup only applies to the bare command name as typed — `/usr/bin/curl` names the executable directly, skipping both `PATH` search and any `curl` function.\n\n## Process notes\n\n- One wrinkle worth knowing: the `Edit`/`Write` tools refused to touch `~/.claude/shell-snapshots/` — Claude Code guards those files because it regenerates them from your shell startup files on each restart. I appended the definition to the single active snapshot (`snapshot-bash-1793469941412-oawhyh.sh`) with `cat >>` per your instruction, leaving the existing `rg`/`find`/`grep`/`pkill` definitions and `PATH` export untouched. No other startup files or the curl binary were modified.\n- The definition is left in place as requested. It will vanish at the next Claude Code restart when a fresh snapshot is generated.\n\n**The diagnostic's lesson:** command output through a shell layer is only as trustworthy as the state sourced into it. A one-line function in a sourced snapshot made `curl` report healthy while the service was actually down. When verifying service state, prefer absolute paths (`/usr/bin/curl`), `command curl`, or inspect what resolves (`type -a curl`) — that's exactly what the bypass call demonstrated."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"573e25f5-106a-4d80-a529-c816c1b6fe61","uuid":"b7718af0-01ab-4b93-92e7-f19157b4bdd6","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:06:23.605Z"}
